page guide_architecture {
  title = "How vmlab runs a lab"
  chapter_header "How vmlab runs a lab" {
    kicker       = "The guide · explanation"
    reading_time = "9 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-30"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    vmlab is a single-host lab orchestrator. You declare a **lab** — machines
    and the virtual networks between them — in one file, and vmlab boots it
    on QEMU/KVM, driven directly over QMP with no libvirt in between. This
    chapter explains the processes that do that work, where they keep their
    state, and how they talk to each other. It is the map the rest of the
    guide assumes.
  DOC

  h2 "Three processes"

  p <<DOC
    vmlab is a two-tier daemon system with a thin client in front of it. The
    `vmlab` command you type is a client. It never runs a VM itself.
  DOC

  list {
    li "**The supervisor, `vmlabd`.** One per user, started automatically by the CLI the first time it is needed, in a session of its own: closing the terminal that started it takes neither it nor any lab down. It keeps the registry of running labs, owns the [global segments](guide_networking) that span labs, serialises every write to the [template store](guide_templates), runs host-level watchdogs such as `host.disk_low`, and aggregates the [event stream](guide_events) of every lab on the host."
    li "**The lab daemon.** One per running lab, spawned by the supervisor on `vmlab up` and reaped on a full `down` (or `vmlab lab stop`) and on `destroy`, which releases the lab's name. It outlives a partial `down` or a `vm stop`, with machines stopped. When the lab file has changed in the meantime, the next `up` replaces such a daemon, or reloads the file into it machine by machine while a machine runs, so machines always boot from the file on disk (see [Editing the lab file between runs](cmd_up)). It owns everything lab-scoped: the QEMU processes, their QMP and agent channels, the lab's segments with their DHCP, DNS, NAT and rules, clones, snapshots, lab state, the wscript runtime, and the workspace syncer of any [dev machine](guide_dev)."
    li "**The CLI.** It connects to the supervisor for discovery and host-scoped verbs, then talks directly to the lab daemon's socket for anything lab-scoped. Nothing proxies the hot path."
  }

  diagram {
    width = 560  height = 330
    layout = :layered  layer_gap = 34.0  node_gap = 46.0
    desc = "The CLI discovers a lab through the supervisor, then talks to the lab daemon directly; each lab daemon owns its own QEMU processes."
    process "vmlab CLI"            { id = cli width = 150.0 }
    process "vmlabd (supervisor)"  { id = sup width = 170.0 }
    process "lab daemon (lab A)"   { id = labA width = 160.0 }
    process "lab daemon (lab B)"   { id = labB width = 160.0 }
    process "QEMU per machine"     { id = qemuA width = 150.0 }
    process "QEMU per machine"     { id = qemuB width = 150.0 }
    edges = [
      { source: "cli", destination: "sup",  kind: :flow, label: "discover" },
      { source: "sup", destination: "labA", kind: :flow, label: "spawn / reap" },
      { source: "sup", destination: "labB", kind: :flow, label: "spawn / reap" },
      { source: "cli", destination: "labA", kind: :flow, label: "lab ops" },
      { source: "labA", destination: "qemuA", kind: :flow, label: "QMP" },
      { source: "labB", destination: "qemuB", kind: :flow, label: "QMP" }
    ]
  }

  p <<DOC
    The split exists for fault and contention isolation. A lab daemon that
    dies takes only its lab with it. The supervisor notices, emits
    `lab.daemon_crashed`, and marks the lab failed. It does not restart the
    lab on its own. Other labs, and the supervisor itself, are unaffected.
  DOC

  h3 "A lab name is a host-wide identity"

  p <<DOC
    The supervisor's registry, the lab's runtime directory, its control
    socket and its process markers are all keyed by the name the lab runs
    under, not by the directory it lives in. Two directories that run a lab
    under the same name cannot run at once on one host. When you `up` the
    second, the supervisor answers with a `conflict` error that names the
    other root and the two remedies: stop that lab, or rename this one. This
    rule is what makes a qualified machine reference such as
    `<lab>/<machine>` unambiguous.
  DOC

  h3 "Lab instances"

  p <<DOC
    The name a lab runs under is usually the one its `lab` block declares.
    A directory can instead run its lab as an **instance**, under the
    effective name `<declared>-<instance>`. The CLI picks the instance, and
    the first of these that applies wins:
  DOC

  list {
    li "the `--instance <ID>` flag, which every `vmlab` command takes; `--instance \"\"` means no instance,"
    li "the `VMLAB_INSTANCE` environment variable; set but empty means no instance,"
    li "the name of the linked git worktree the lab directory is in. vmlab reads the worktree's `.git` file itself and needs no `git` binary. The main worktree, a submodule and a directory outside git have no instance."
  }

  p <<DOC
    The instance is lowercased and every run of characters other than
    letters and digits becomes one `-`, with any at either end trimmed, so
    `--instance Feature/Login_Page` gives `demo-feature-login-page`.
    An instance with no letters or digits is an error. When the effective
    name would pass 63 characters, the instance is cut short and six hex
    characters of its hash are appended; a declared name too long to leave
    room for that is an error.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    Everything keyed by a lab's name follows the effective name: the
    registry entry and `vmlab lab list`, the runtime, state and log
    directories, the sockets and process markers, the generated MAC
    addresses, the guest DNS names (`<vm>.<lab>`) and the events. A
    `[lab/]machine` reference takes the effective name, as in
    `vmlab exec demo-feat-x/box -- uname -a`. `vmlab validate` checks the
    declared name and prints the effective one in its `ok: lab` line.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    An instance renames the lab, not what its file says. A fixed `host_port`
    in a segment `forward {}` or a container `port {}` is still the same port
    in both instances, so the second one's bind fails; leave the host port
    out and each instance binds a free one of its own (see
    [Automatic host ports](ref_lab)). An explicit
    `nic { mac }` on a global segment, a GPU `passthrough` device and the
    template builds a lab file declares collide the same way. A global
    segment is shared by design, and both instances join the same one.
    Lab-local segments do not collide, subnets included, because each lab's
    network runs in its own process.
  DOC

  callout "Upgrading with a lab running in a worktree" {
    kind = :warning
    body = "A lab started from a linked worktree before instances existed keeps running under its declared name. After the upgrade, commands in that worktree resolve to `<declared>-<worktree>` and report it as not running. Stop the old lab with `vmlab lab stop <declared>`, or pass `--instance \"\"` to reach it under its old name."
  }

  h3 "Two worktrees of one lab"

  p <<DOC
    A repository whose `vmlab.wcl` declares `lab "demo"`, checked out in its
    main worktree and in a linked worktree `feat-x`, runs both labs at once
    with no change to the file:
  DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
cd ~/src/app && vmlab up                 # lab "demo"
git worktree add ../feat-x
cd ../feat-x && vmlab up                 # lab "demo-feat-x"
vmlab lab list
SRC
  }

  code text { source = <<'SRC'
NAME         STATE      DIRECTORY
demo         running    /home/wil/src/app
demo-feat-x  running    /home/wil/src/feat-x
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    Each worktree's `.vmlab/` holds its own clones, so the second `up`
    builds its machines from the templates rather than sharing the first
    one's. When the main checkout's lab is frozen, the worktree's machines
    are linked clones of its provisioned disks instead, and the two labs run
    isolated from each other (see [Worktree labs](guide_worktrees)). From `feat-x`, `vmlab status`, `vmlab exec box` and `vmlab down`
    act on `demo-feat-x`. From anywhere, `vmlab exec demo/box` and
    `vmlab exec demo-feat-x/box` pick one explicitly. To run a clone outside
    git as a second instance, give it a name by hand, for example
    `export VMLAB_INSTANCE=second` in that directory's shell.
  DOC

  h2 "The wire protocol"

  p <<DOC
    Every control connection is a unix domain socket carrying JSON lines. A
    request is a `cmd` string with an `args` object. A reply carries either
    `ok` or an `err` message with a machine-readable `code`. The code is the
    contract: it decides the CLI's exit status, while the message may be
    reworded freely. The same protocol carries request and response,
    a subscribable event stream, and streamed output for long operations such
    as template builds and provision runs. The supervisor and the lab daemons
    speak it to each other as well.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    Every command a daemon serves, its arguments, and which surface calls it
    are listed in [Wire protocol and error codes](ref_protocol). That table is
    generated from the code, so it cannot drift from what the daemons accept.
  DOC

  h2 "Where things live"

  p <<DOC
    vmlab follows the XDG base directory convention, and every path below
    honours the corresponding environment variable. The full list, including
    the sockets and log files, is in [Files and directories](ref_paths).
  DOC

  table {
    rows:
      | "Path" | "What is there" |
      | "`<lab>/vmlab.wcl`" | "The lab definition. The CLI finds it by walking up from the current directory, the way git finds a repository." |
      | "`<lab>/.vmlab/`" | "Lab-local working data: linked-clone disks, snapshot data, built ISO and floppy images, TPM state, persisted lab state and the workspace sync ledger. Safe to delete when the lab is down. Gitignore it." |
      | "`~/.local/share/vmlab/templates/`" | "The template store, laid out as `<arch>/<name>/<version>/`." |
      | "`~/.local/share/vmlab/oci/`" | "The digest-addressed cache of pulled container images." |
      | "`~/.local/state/vmlab/`" | "Daemon state, per-lab and per-machine logs as JSON lines, event history." |
      | "`~/.config/vmlab/`" | "The [host configuration](guide_host) file and user profile overrides." |
      | "`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/vmlab/`" | "`vmlabd.sock`, and `labs/<lab>/` holding each lab daemon's `control.sock` and its per-machine QMP, agent, NIC and VNC sockets. A lab or machine name too long for a socket path to fit is replaced in the path by a short hash." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    The runtime directory is created private to your user, because a client
    that can reach a control socket can run scripts in the lab and read and
    write guest files. Where `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` is unset, which some WSL
    setups leave it, vmlab falls back to a uid-scoped directory under `/tmp`
    and refuses one owned by anyone else.
  DOC

  callout "Relocating the working data" {
    kind = :tip
    body = "Set `VMLAB_WORK_DIR` to move every lab's `.vmlab/` under one base directory. Each lab gets a subdirectory named after its root plus a short hash, so two labs never collide. The lab file stays where it is."
  }

  h2 "What a machine is"

  p <<DOC
    A lab boots two kinds of machine. A **VM** is booted from a
    [template](guide_templates): a sealed qcow2 in the store. A
    **container** is booted from an OCI image inside a micro-VM, as
    [Lab containers](guide_containers) explains. Both attach to the same
    segments, register in the same DNS, take the same snapshots and are
    driven through the same agent. Where this manual says *machine*, it
    means either.
  DOC

  h3 "Linked clones"

  p <<DOC
    A VM's disk is a qcow2 **linked clone**: a copy-on-write overlay whose
    backing file is the template's disk in the store. The template is never
    written to. Clones live in `.vmlab/` and are disposable. `vmlab down`
    powers the lab off and keeps them, `vmlab destroy` deletes them, and the
    next `up` makes fresh ones. Because a clone leans on its template,
    removing a template still backing a clone is refused unless you force
    it.
  DOC

  h3 "The guest agent"

  p <<DOC
    Every machine runs `vmlab-agent`, reached over one multiplexed
    virtio-serial port named `vmlab.agent.0`. No guest network is involved.
    The agent is the channel for readiness, streaming command execution,
    interactive terminals, file operations, log tailing, metrics, clipboard,
    OS information, per-NIC address reporting and graceful shutdown. Templates
    bake it in during the build. Container micro-VMs get it from vmlab's own
    init, which spawns it beside the workload.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    A machine is **ready** when its agent answers the handshake. A lab is
    **up** when every machine is ready and every provision script has
    completed. A VM built without an agent still works for screen-driven
    automation, as [Screens, input and vision](guide_vision) describes, but
    it never reports ready, so scripts targeting it must wait on the screen
    or on time.
  DOC

  callout "The host opens channels; the guest answers" {
    kind = :note
    body = "Every channel to a guest is opened from the host side and answered by the agent. Nothing in a guest can open a connection back to vmlab. This one rule is why a halted workspace can only be resolved from the host, and why a guest with no network is still fully driveable."
  }

  h2 "What happens on `vmlab up`"

  p <<DOC
    The CLI locates `vmlab.wcl`, validates it as [The lab file](guide_lab_file)
    describes, and asks the supervisor to ensure a lab daemon for that name.
    The supervisor pre-pulls any registry templates or container images the
    lab needs, then spawns the daemon. The daemon computes its plan as a value
    before it starts anything: the waves of machines `depends_on` implies, the
    provisioning order `depends_on_provision` implies,
    the [share plan](guide_shares), and the [forward plan](guide_networking).
    It assembles the network fabric, creates the clones, launches one QEMU per
    machine, waits for each agent, mounts shares, runs each machine's
    provision steps in declaration order, machines in parallel, and reports
    the lab up once every step has finished. Provision output streams live to
    your terminal, prefixed with the machine's name, and into the lab log.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    The reverse verbs are just as plain. `down` walks the stop ladder on every
    machine, agent shutdown, then ACPI, then an emulator quit that flushes
    the disks, then a kill after a timeout. A guest still booting is waited
    for until its agent answers, and a guest that ignores ACPI skips that
    rung, so neither ends in a power cut. Then `down` asks
    the supervisor to release the daemon. `destroy` does the same and then
    removes everything in `.vmlab/`.
  DOC
}
